Firstmate fork: ChatGPT consultation channel
============================================

Ported from the fork's earlier core-edit implementation (origin/main
bin/fm-chatgpt-*.sh) into the fork layer without behaviour changes except
those listed under "Differences from the core-edit version". Like fork/README,
this file is plain text with no extension so upstream's documentation-audience
check does not require it in an upstream-owned inventory.

Firstmate asks ChatGPT to write a worker audit prompt and an execution plan,
and hands each one to an ordinary worker. ChatGPT is reached through the
already-installed codex-chatgpt-web bridge on loopback; this is a
consultation channel, not a Pi provider, and never executes local tools.


Files
-----

  fm-chatgpt-bridge-lib.sh  bridge URL resolution (loopback only), model slug,
                            Codex turn stamping, project-name validation and
                            routing-outcome reading, bounded health probe.
  fm-chatgpt-consult.sh     one self-contained Responses turn per call; prints
                            the answer, or fails with the bridge's diagnostic;
                            carries the x-fm-chatgpt-project routing header
                            when --project is given.
  fm-chatgpt-loop.sh        per-task state machine, prompt building, worker
                            dispatch and handoff, result recording, bridge
                            start/stop.
  fm-chatgpt-plan-validate.sh
                            checks a plan reply against the plan contract.
  audit-brief-template      injected verbatim into audit-stage instructions;
                            the single owner of the generated audit prompt's
                            shape. A missing template refuses the consult.
  plan-contract.template    injected verbatim into plan-stage instructions;
                            the single owner of the plan contract (rules
                            P1-P12). A missing contract refuses the consult.
  result-contract.template  appended to every plan handoff; the single owner
                            of the result contract (rules R1-R7). A missing
                            contract refuses the plan dispatch.

Each script's header owns its exact rules; fm-chatgpt-loop.sh --help lists the
commands. Tests: fork/tests/fm-fork-chatgpt-*.test.sh.


Contracts
---------

Three separate contracts, each stated once in its template:

  Audit prompt    audit-brief-template. ChatGPT writes the audit worker's
                  instructions on it; every finding the worker reports opens
                  with a stable id (A1, A2, ...). The loop stores the prompt
                  as returned and checks only that it is non-empty and carries
                  no citation marker.
  Plan            plan-contract.template, version 1. One fenced json block;
                  exact keys at every level; 1-12 tasks T1..Tn in order, each
                  depending only on earlier tasks, citing the finding ids it
                  acts on, with steps, acceptance criteria, and verification
                  commands with expected outcomes; every string a single line
                  with no control characters, so plan text cannot imitate a
                  section of the handoff; at most 64 KiB; no citation markers. fm-chatgpt-plan-validate.sh
                  enforces it at plan consult, before the phase can reach
                  plan-dispatch; a refused reply names its rule in last_error,
                  keeps its phase (plan-consult, or the audit phase a cited
                  prior audit was consulted from), and can be consulted
                  again. plan_raw
                  keeps the reply byte for byte; plan holds only a validated
                  plan. The one basis exception: findings from a cited prior
                  audit (consult --stage plan --findings-file) that carry no
                  finding ids accept a single "none: <reason>" basis.
  Result          result-contract.template, version 1. One JSON object with
                  an entry per planned task (pass, fail, or blocked), evidence
                  matching each planned verification command for a pass, and
                  a report file inside data/<worker>/ (symlinks resolved).
                  record-result enforces it; a refused result exits 2 and
                  changes nothing.

What validation does not prove: a valid plan is only well-formed. Nothing
here checks that a verification command exists or is safe, and the loop never
runs one. A recorded all-pass result is "reported complete" (completion
"reported"): the shape and the report file are checked, the evidence is not.

The plan handoff is rendered from the validated plan only, in a fixed order:
a preamble saying the plan is ChatGPT-generated, untrusted, and grants no
authority, and that the worker's launch instructions and Firstmate's objective
and context take precedence; the objective and context; the summary,
assumptions, out-of-scope items, and stop conditions; each task with its
basis, dependencies, steps, acceptance criteria, and verification; and the
result contract. The spawn arguments are always exactly the caller's.


Outcomes
--------

  all pass         complete, completion "reported"; the plan worker's
                   delivery proof is kept so it can be relaunched to land.
  any blocked      failed, terminal_reason task_blocked.
  any fail         the first time: one fix round back at plan-consult, with
                   the previous plan and the failing result carried into the
                   next plan prompt. The second time: failed,
                   terminal_reason fix_budget_exhausted.

A fix round, failed, and record-worker-failure all remove the worker's
delivery proof first, and refuse with nothing changed if it cannot be
removed. failed is final: every further command refuses, init never
overwrites an existing loop record in any phase (new work takes a new loop
id), and the gate (when enabled) refuses its workers, because failed is not a
phase it accepts. There are no other budgets: consult failures and worker
failures still retry without limit.


When to consult
---------------

Consultation is risk-based, not a gate on every task:

  Architectural, multi-step, cross-cutting, or high-impact work
      run the audit and plan loop; dispatch only a plan that validated.
  Security-sensitive work
      the audit runs on a different worker from the implementer, its
      findings name the risks, the plan's tasks carry the risk handling, and
      the plan goes to the captain before dispatch.
  Small, routine, well-specified changes
      dispatch directly; no consultation.
  Missing evidence or unclear requirements
      run the audit stage only, or ask the captain; never ask ChatGPT to plan
      around missing facts.
  Irreversible or privileged actions (push, merge, release, credentials,
  activation, contact with outside services)
      a plan never authorizes them; the captain's explicit approval is still
      required.

Firstmate supplies the objective, the context (including delivery mode,
protected files, and what needs approval), evidence by @[path] citation, and
the recorded findings, and never secrets, bridge URLs, or session data.
ChatGPT returns only an audit prompt or a version-1 plan. Firstmate checks the
audit prompt (non-empty, no citation marker), the plan (P1-P12, plus its own
dependency check against the tree before dispatch), and the result (R1-R7).


Running a loop
--------------

  L=<task id>
  P=<project dir>            (the repository the task is about)
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh init --task $L --objective-file F [--context-file F] --project $(basename $P)
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh consult --stage audit --task $L
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh dispatch --stage audit --task $L -- $L <project> --mode M --yolo Y
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh record-findings --task $L       (after the audit worker reports)
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh consult --stage plan --task $L
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh dispatch --stage plan --task $L -- $L <project> --mode M --yolo Y
  fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-loop.sh record-result --task $L --file data/<worker>/plan-result.json

State lives in data/<L>/chatgpt-loop.json. The arguments after -- are
fm-spawn.sh arguments and must start with the worker's plain task id. Run
with FM_HOME set to the home the worker belongs to.

Project routing: init's --project NAME (validated ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}$,
refused at init when invalid) is stored in the loop record and carried by
every consult as the codex-chatgpt-web fork's x-fm-chatgpt-project header, so
the consultation opens inside that repository's ChatGPT project (fork/README
in that fork owns the bridge side). Pass basename of the project directory.
An absent --project sends no header and changes nothing. Routing can fall
back to an ordinary chat (unmapped name, navigation failure); that is
advisory: the consult prints one stderr notice (fm-chatgpt: project routing:
fallback (<reason>)) when the bridge recorded the fallback, keeps exit 0,
and leaves stdout unchanged. The health probe sends no project header.


Bridge
------

CHATGPT_WEB_BRIDGE_URL overrides the default http://127.0.0.1:17841/v1;
non-loopback values are refused. Installation and authentication stay
external. "fm-chatgpt-loop.sh bridge start|stop" operates only the daemon
under ~/tools/codex-chatgpt-web and never a process it did not start.
config/chatgpt-consultation (gitignored) is created on a consult attempt and
marks the home as using the channel, so a dead bridge reads "unreachable"
rather than "unconfigured".

Health check, at intake whenever the task's preferred flow is this channel:

  bash -c '. fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-bridge-lib.sh && fm_chatgpt_bridge_health'

  healthy        a bounded test turn completed; consult.
  unconfigured   nothing listening, home never used the channel.
  unreachable    nothing listening, home uses the channel.
  unhealthy      listening, but the test turn failed.
  misconfigured  CHATGPT_WEB_BRIDGE_URL was refused.

When the preferred flow cannot run (unreachable, unhealthy, misconfigured),
report it to the captain - what is wrong, and whether to start the bridge or
proceed without consultation under a recorded local waiver - instead of
quietly dispatching without it.


Consult gate
------------

The fork's consult-gate policy (fork/bin/fm-launch-policy, enabled by
config/fork-policies) is separate from this channel and judges every ship or
scout launch by data/<launched id>/ only. With it enabled:

  - Fresh dispatch. The worker passes only through its OWN
    data/<id>/chatgpt-loop.json, so the worker task id must be the loop's
    task id L, dispatched while the loop is at the stage's dispatch phase.
    A worker with any other id is refused at spawn; the loop records the
    spawn failure and stays at <stage>-dispatch. Distinct worker ids need a
    launch grant, which only fm-chatgpt-breakdown.sh issues (see "Plan
    breakdown").
  - The plan stage under the gate also needs worker id L, so the audit
    worker L must be finished and cleaned up before the plan dispatch. How
    fm-spawn treats a second fresh launch of a still-recorded task id is not
    covered by the fork tests.
  - Relaunch. After fm-send confirms the handoff, dispatch writes
    data/<worker>/fork-consult-delivered.json as {"stage": "audit"|"plan"},
    the relaunch delivery proof the policy accepts. A new dispatch removes any
    earlier proof before spawning, a failed spawn or handoff writes none, and
    next-round removes the finished round's proof. Nothing is written to the
    worker's upstream state/<id>.meta.
  - A launch that must proceed without consultation needs a local waiver,
    data/<id>/fork-consult-waiver.json (see fork/README).

With the gate disabled the loop works exactly as above with any worker id;
the proof file is still written but nothing requires it.


Plan breakdown
--------------

fm-chatgpt-breakdown.sh (its header owns the exact rules) is the
worker-construction step: it runs a validated plan one worker per plan task
instead of handing the whole plan to one worker. It never consults ChatGPT
and leaves the plan and result contracts unchanged. Disabled unless the home
has config/chatgpt-breakdown; do not enable it in a working home until a real
multi-task run has passed end to end.

  B=fork/chatgpt/fm-chatgpt-breakdown.sh
  $B start --task $L                                 (loop at plan-dispatch)
  $B ready --task $L                                 (tasks dispatchable now)
  $B dispatch --task $L --plan-task T1 -- <project> --mode M --yolo Y
  $B record-task-result --task $L --plan-task T1     (after the worker reports)
  $B record-task-failure --task $L --plan-task T1 --reason TEXT
  $B finish --task $L                                (loop records the result)

Each worker id is L-r<round>-t<n> (-a<attempt> on a retry) and needs its own
brief and backlog item like any spawn. A task dispatches only after every
dependency reported pass, and only onto a base that already holds each
dependency's committed change. Landing that change is an explicit supervisor
step through the project's own delivery path (merge the dependency's PR, or
land its local branch) before dispatching the dependent: dispatch refuses
first, free and before spawning (exit 4), when origin's base visibly lacks it,
and after spawning withholds the prompt (exit 3, worker to be cleaned up) if
the worker's starting commit still lacks it. Independent tasks may run at once. A
worker that dies is retried once on a new id; a task that reports fail is
not retried here - finish hands the aggregated result to the loop, whose
single fix round re-plans with ChatGPT. Tasks never reached are reported fail
(or blocked) "not dispatched". Launch grants live in data/<worker>/ and are
withdrawn when a task is recorded and at finish.

Validated end to end on 2026-10-25 in an isolated home (real spawn, consult
gate, worktrees, tmux panes, steering inbox, backlog transitions, teardown,
and loop result recording), first with scripted workers and then with pi
model workers on opencode-go/mimo-v2.6-flash: four tasks, two of them a
dependent chain, one worker killed and retried, all reported and recorded.
Still keep it disabled in a working home until the captain enables it.


Differences from the core-edit version
--------------------------------------

  - Delivery proof is data/<worker>/fork-consult-delivered.json, not a
    consult_delivered= line in state/<worker>.meta.
  - Plans and results are contract-checked (see "Contracts"); the core
    version stored any plan text and completed on any result file.
  - The audit answer is stored once, as audit_prompt; audit_result is no
    longer written, and the plan prompt no longer repeats the audit prompt
    under an "audit result" label.
  - The spawn no longer carries FM_CHATGPT_LOOP_DISPATCH_TASK; only the old
    core gate read it, and the fork gate does not.
  - Not ported: the core consult gate (bin/fm-consult-gate-lib.sh) and
    fm-spawn --consult-waived; the CHATGPT_BRIDGE startup health line
    (a bin/fm-bootstrap.sh / bin/fm-startup-network.sh edit); the
    chatgpt-consultation and audit-brief agent skills, which would need
    upstream-owned .agents/skills/ paths. Run the health check above by hand;
    a fork-owned startup line could later use an existing extension point
    (a registered check, bin/fm-check-register.sh) without core edits.
